Let me write the fix:

```rust
pub fn parse_status_files(porcelain: &str) -> Vec<(String, String)> {
    porcelain
        .lines()
        .filter_map(|line| {
            if line.len() < 4 {
                return None;
            }
            let status = line[..2].trim().to_string();
            // Porcelain format: XY <space> filename (or XY <space> old -> new for renames)
            let raw = line[3..].trim();
            // For renames/copies, porcelain shows "old -> new"; the real filename is the target.
            let filename = match raw.split_once(" -> ") {
                Some((_, new_name)) => new_name.trim().to_string(),
                None => raw.to_string(),
            };
            if filename.is_empty() {
                return None;
            }
            Some((status, filename))
        })
        .collect()
}
```

Wait — should I use `split_once(" -> ")`? The porcelain format uses " -> " between old and new. Yes. And the new name could itself contain " -> "? Extremely unlikely (a filename literally containing " -> " — possible in theory!). To be safe, use `rsplit_once` — take the last " -> " as the separator so filenames containing " -> " still work. Actually for `old -> new`, old could contain " -> " too but not in practice. Using `rsplit_once` on the whole string: if the actual filename contains " -> ", e.g., rename `a -> b` → `b -> c`: string is `a -> b -> c`. `split_once` gives ("a", "b -> c") — new = "b -> c" ✓. `rsplit_once` gives ("a -> b", "c") — also "c" ✓. Both give the right answer here actually, since the new filename is last. `rsplit_once` is the safer choice when the *new* name contains " -> ". Use `rsplit_once`.

